+//
+// DADAO (SimRISC) ELF linker target.
+//
+// Relocation model (contract-elf.md §2–§4, ADR-0019 D10, contract-dynlink.md
+// §4.3):
+//   R_DADAO_NONE (0): no-op placeholder (not part of dynamic-section semantics).
+//   R_DADAO_ABS48 (6): absolute 48-bit address.  Two encodings share the type:
+//       * instruction (set.zw/or.w/andn.w in an executable section): one
+//         16-bit wyde per instruction, selected by wyde-position (wp = hb[5:4]);
+//       * data (`.dd.o64 <sym>` / `.quad <sym>`, non-executable section): a
+//         single 8-byte absolute address field.
+//   R_DADAO_REL26 (1): field = (S + A - P) >> 2, imms24 (effective 26 bits)
+//   R_DADAO_REL20 (2): field = (S + A - P) >> 2, imms18 (effective 20 bits)
+//   R_DADAO_REL14 (3): field = (S + A - P) >> 2, imms12 (effective 14 bits)
+//   R_DADAO_REL12 (4): field = S + A - P, imms12 (BYTE offset, no >>2).  Used by
+//       ld.*/st.* with base rb0 (PC-relative: rb0 reads the current instruction
+//       address, so P is the reloc site itself).
+//   R_DADAO_ABS12 (5): field = S + A, imms12 (BYTE offset, no >>2).  Used by
+//       ld.*/st.* with base rb1..rb63 (base-relative/absolute).
+//   R_DADAO_PCREL_HI (14) / R_DADAO_PCREL_LO (15): the PC-relative hi/lo pair
+//       (ADR-0029 D2, contract-elf.md §2.2/§3.1).  The high half patches the
+//       `aip.si` imms18 field, the low half the paired `ld.o` imms12 field;
+//       both derive from the *same* (S + A - P), where P is the `aip.si` site
+//       address.  The high field is rounded to the nearest 4 KiB multiple
+//       ((S + A - P + 0x800) >> 12) and the low field is the signed residual,
+//       so the reach is ±512 MiB and no `.got`-beside-`.text` layout is needed
+//       (ADR-0029 D3/D4).  The low record carries the *pairing information*:
+//       its symbol+addend resolve to the site address, whose own record is the
+//       high half (RISC-V's `%pcrel_lo(label)` form) -- the linker therefore
+//       recovers the high half's target instead of using the low record's
+//       symbol as a data address.
+//   R_DADAO_JUMP_SLOT (7): PLT GOT slot, 8-byte field, S + A unsigned 48-bit
+//       (musl REL_PLT).
+//   R_DADAO_RELATIVE (8): 8-byte slot, B + A unsigned 48-bit (musl
+//       REL_RELATIVE; B = load bias, added by the loader).
+//   R_DADAO_TLS_DTPMOD (10) / R_DADAO_TLS_DTPREL (11) / R_DADAO_TLS_TPREL (12):
+//       8-byte slots carrying the module TLS id, the module-relative offset and
+//       the TP-relative offset (contract-dynlink.md §4.3, ADR-0019 D10).  The
+//       compiler emits them as `.got` slot contents for the dynamic TLS models
+//       (initial-exec / global-dynamic, LLVM-117t); scanSection() decides
+//       between a link-time constant and a dynamic relocation.
+//   R_DADAO_COPY (9) / R_DADAO_TLSDESC (13): reserved by ADR-0019 D10 but
+//       **not emitted** by this implementation; encountering one is an explicit
+//       error (ADR-0026 D7②, contract-dynlink.md §10②).
+//
+// Dynamic output (contract-dynlink.md §3/§5/§6, ADR-0026 D2–D6): the DADAO
+// target produces ET_DYN via lld's generic machinery —
+//   * a data/GOT slot holding a symbol address is a dynamic relocation of type
+//     ABS48 (REL_SYMBOLIC/REL_GOT) against the symbol, or RELATIVE when the
+//     symbol is non-preemptible (the module-relative address is the addend);
+//   * calls/jumps (REL26) to preemptible or undefined functions go through a
+//     PLT entry (R_PLT_PC), so a shared object resolves them through
+//     .got.plt/JUMP_SLOT;
+//   * binding is **eager** (ADR-0026 D6): there is no lazy resolver, hence no
+//     PLT header, and every slot is loader-filled before the program starts.
+//
+// v5 has **no -4** pipeline offset: rb0 reads the address of the current
+// instruction, so the PC-relative displacement is measured from P itself
+// (contract-elf.md §3.1, ADR-0019 §D3).
+//
+// Out-of-range results are a link-time error (no truncation, no wrap;
+// contract-elf.md §3.2, ADR-0019 §D6).  Relaxation is disabled: the linker
+// only patches in place, never shortens or reorders (ADR-0019 §D7).
+//
+// One case is resolved instead of rejected (LLVM-085t): a PC-relative
+// branch/jump/call whose symbol is an *undefined weak* function.  ELF gives it
+// the value 0, which is unencodable from a 0xFC00_0000_0000 image (and no
+// veneer can bridge to address 0 either).  Following the RISC-V convention for
+// relative-only ISAs, the target becomes the place itself (self-loop); a `call`
+// is rewritten to `jump [rb0, 0]` so no RegRAS entry is pushed for a callee
+// that cannot pop it.  Unimplemented optional hooks are then a visible hang
+// rather than a link error.  Strong/defined out-of-range symbols are still
+// rejected, and the non-branch REL12 path is untouched.
+//
+// The instruction-vs-data distinction uses the target section's SHF_EXECINSTR
+// flag (ADR-0019 §D5: "指令逐条挂 reloc ... 数据为单条 8 字节字段 reloc").
+//
+//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
